      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Pelvic instability is a term describing a group of conditions causing pelvic girdle pain in pregnancy. Although a common problem in pregnancy, it is easily overlooked and sometimes dismissed as 'normal' by primary care professionals."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "This article outlines the clinical features, diagnosis and management of pelvic instability in pregnancy."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "DISCUSSION",
          "content": "General practitioners are in an ideal position to diagnose pelvic instability, implement treatment, and refer for further care to a physiotherapist with an interest in women's health. Use of a supportive belt or taping, appropriate analgesia, core stability and pelvic floor exercises, and avoiding heavy lifting and activities that exacerbate the pain are the mainstays of treatment."
        }
      ]},
